########################################################

              README for SMOG 2 (v2.7beta)

SMOG 2 is a downloadable software package for 
generating structure-based models, as provided by the 
smog-server.org webserver (i.e. SMOG v1). These models 
may be used to perform simulations using Gromacs, NAMD,
or OpenMM.

SMOG 2 is free software, distributed under the GNU 
General Public License. See COPYING.

This distribution of SMOG 2 includes two versions of 
SMOG models, the C-alpha model (Clementi et al. JMB 2000)
and the all-atom model (Whitford et al. Proteins 2009),
as well as two definitions of contacts (LJ, Gaussians).
The software is written such that you may also design
your own form of structure-based models without having
to modify the source code. For even greater flexibility,
we recommend checking the -OpenSMOG option, which 
provides a platform for performing SMOG models in OpenMM.

This README provides the absolute basics for using
SMOG 2.  For more advanced usage information, consult
the manual and tutorials, available at smog-server.org

SMOG3 STATUS IN THIS REPOSITORY

This branch also contains SMOG3, a Python-native successor
implementation under src/smog3.  Normal SMOG3 runtime does
not invoke Perl.  Java is used where needed for the existing
SCM contact-generation workflow.

Latest verified release-readiness status:

- Python tests: 95 passed.
- Selected two-stage parity: all selected cases OK.
- Original drop-in SMOG-CHECK harness: PASSED TESTS 1 to 115.
- SMOG3 runtime: Python plus Java only, zero Perl.

The original SMOG-CHECK harness is itself Perl.  In the drop-in
test, its `smog2` calls are routed through a temporary SMOG3-backed
wrapper with `SMOG3_LEGACY_PERL_FALLBACK=0`; the full run wrapper
log recorded 138 SMOG3 invocations.

The SMOG-CHECK-style two-stage parity campaign still runs original
SMOG2 only inside the official smogserver/smog2:stable Docker image
and runs SMOG3 locally with Python plus Java.  Its latest verified
result is 110 passing runnable baseline cases, 0 SMOG3 failures,
0 unsupported cases, and 5 BASELINE_ERROR cases where the official
SMOG2 Docker image cannot generate complete baseline outputs for
the current repository templates.  Those baseline errors are cases
104, 110, 111, 112, and 113.

Useful SMOG3 verification commands:

>PYTHONPATH=src pytest -q tests
>bash scripts/run_selected_two_stage_parity.sh
>bash scripts/run_smogcheck_dropin_smog3.sh 1 115

Install the alpha package from TestPyPI with:

>python3 -m pip install --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ --extra-index-url https://pypi.org/simple/ smog3

After installation, check the command-line entry point with:

>smog3 --help

The TestPyPI package exposes `smog3` as the main Python-native runtime command
and `smog3-parity-direct` for direct comparator diagnostics.  It intentionally
does not expose a default public `smog2` command; drop-in SMOG-CHECK testing
uses `scripts/run_smogcheck_dropin_smog3.sh` from a source checkout to create
a temporary compatibility wrapper.

Generated parity reports and output directories are local
artifacts and should not be committed.  See PARITY.md for the
full status table, comparator policy, drop-in SMOG-CHECK harness
notes, and baseline-error notes.

We encourage everyone to provide feedback/suggestions
on usage, extensions and bugs to info@smog-server.org,
or post questions to the users forum.  You can sign up
for the users forum at
https://mailman.rice.edu/mailman/listinfo/smog-users


      Jeffrey Noel, Jose' Onuchic and Paul Whitford

